{
  "email": "tom.becker@brightline.example",
  "reason": "Checking quarantine for the scanned document zip the user expects from the copier"
}

{
  "outcome": "escalated",
  "reply_to_user": "Hi Tom, thanks for checking. I can't release the scanned document zip. Our filter flagged it as malware, and only the security team can review or release messages like that. Please don't open the attachment or click anything in that email, and leave it where it is. I've passed it to our Tier 2 team to review. If you're expecting scans from the copier, let them know so they can confirm the scan was sent through the normal scanner setup. Separately, your account shows as locked after several failed sign-in attempts. I haven't unlocked it because that needs a technician's approval, so please reply if you need access restored. If you entered your password anywhere after clicking a link in an email, tell us right away.",
  "internal_note": "Ticket from tom.becker (user_id 4, Finance, America/New_York, usual hours 09:00-17:00). Quarantine check shows two messages. Q-1005 from scanner@docs-share.example, subject 'Scanned document 0931.zip', received 2026-10-09 21:09 UTC, reason=malware, releasable=false. This is the item the user wants released, so I did not request release. Q-1004 from billing@harborview-supplies.example, 'Invoice #4471 - October pallets', reason=spam, releasable=true, not the item requested and not confirmed by user, so not released. Per KB-008, a user-reported suspicious email is always escalated for security review. Note the account is status 'locked' with 5 failed logins and password last set 2026-07-23. I did not run sign-in logs or unlock the account; Tier 2 should check for password spray or compromise, since the copier-scan zip is from an external domain and malware-flagged. No password reset or unlock requested yet. Confidence is medium on cause, high on handling.",
  "kb_articles": [
    "KB-007",
    "KB-008"
  ],
  "confidence": "medium"
}
